      "id": 1,
      "text": "Originally called Gauglersville, the town became Martinsdale in 1878, named after Montana delegate Martin Maginnis who helped secure a post office.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martinsdale%2C_Montana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Martinsdale once hosted a station on the Chicago, Milwaukee, St. Paul and Pacific Railroad, a key transcontinental line now abandoned.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martinsdale%2C_Montana",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town grew around sheep ranching, flanked by the massive Smith ranch to the west and the Bair spread to the east.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitmt.com/cities-towns/martinsdale",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The former Bair family home, once home to one of America's largest sheep operations, now serves as the Bair Family Museum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
